7. Rendering Objects

This works

{} && <h1>Hello</h1>

Because JavaScript first evaluates it to

<h1>Hello</h1>

React never tries to render the object.

This does not work

<div>
    {{}}
</div>

Error

Objects are not valid as a React child.

Because React is trying to render the object itself.

8. Common Mistake
const items=[];

return(
    <div>
        {items.length && <List/>}
    </div>
)

items.length

0

JavaScript returns

0

React renders

0

Correct way

{items.length > 0 && <List/>}

Now JavaScript returns

false

React ignores false, so nothing is rendered.

9. Interview Summary
Why can't we use if...else inside JSX?

JSX only accepts JavaScript expressions inside {} because expressions evaluate to a value. if, for, while, and switch are statements, so they don't produce a value that JSX can render. To use conditional rendering, perform the logic before return, assign JSX to a variable, use the ternary operator, logical &&, or wrap the logic inside an IIFE.
